    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function",
    "problem": "If $h(x)=8{x}^{6}+4{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-12,$ find ${({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${h}^{-1}(x).$",
    "worked_solution": "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=8{x}^{6}+4{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-12,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=8{y}^{6}+4{y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}-12\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={h}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(8{y}^{6}+4{y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}-12) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =48{y}^{5}+16{y}^{3}+9{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{48{y}^{5}+16{y}^{3}+9{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${h}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={h}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{h}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{48({h}^{-1}(x))^{5}+16({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+9({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}} \\\\ {({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{48({h}^{-1}(x))^{5}+16({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+9({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
